      body: 'Jalen was in tenth grade and annoyed. His world history textbook had a two-page spread on ancient Egypt, beautiful pictures of the pyramids at Giza, and a map that put Egypt in a box with Greece and Mesopotamia, colored the same pale blue as Europe. Africa below it was blank tan. He showed his grandmother at the kitchen table at Greater Mount Olive, where she ran the after-school program in the fellowship hall. “Grandma, look. They act like Egypt floated over from somewhere.” She did not get upset. She asked him a question. “What does the book say its proof is?” Jalen flipped. No footnote. No source. It just said it. “Then you have homework,” she said, and pulled two library books out of her tote bag: a UNESCO General History of Africa volume, and a worn copy of a book by a man named Cheikh Anta Diop. They read together for an hour. She showed him the report of a meeting in Cairo in 1974, where the experts themselves wrote that the discussion had a real lack of balance because not everyone had come as prepared as Diop and Obenga. She also showed him where the same report said most of the experts did not accept all of Diop’s claims. “We are not trading one story for another,” she said. “We read what everybody wrote, and we check it.” Then she opened her Bible to Genesis 10 and showed him Mizraim, the Hebrew name for Egypt, listed beside Cush among the sons of Ham. “The Word put Egypt in Africa’s family a long time before any textbook,” she said. Jalen wrote his history paper on that map. He cited the UNESCO report, page by page. He also wrote one paragraph on where Diop went too far, about the Bible being copied from Egypt, and answered it from 2 Peter 1:21. His teacher wrote one line on the paper: Best-sourced essay in the class. Where did you learn to do this? Jalen grinned and told his grandmother that night. She just smiled, opened to Luke 8, and put her finger on verse 17 without a word: hidden things come out, if somebody will go and look.',
