      title: 'The Missing Word',
      body: 'Professor Elaine Whitfield taught African American history at a small HBCU in North Carolina, and every fall she gave the same assignment on the first day. She handed out a single printed sentence, copied from a popular website, describing what a famous scholar had said about Cheikh Anta Diop in 1961: that Diop’s hypothesis had been presented without supporting data. Then she handed out a photocopy of the same passage as Mercer Cook printed it in 1974, in the translator’s preface to The African Origin of Civilization. The printed page said the hypothesis was not presented without supporting data. One word. “Find the difference,” she said. It usually took a few minutes. Then someone would gasp. One small word had been dropped, and the meaning had turned upside down. “This is how a record gets changed,” she told them. “Not always with a fire or a law. Sometimes with one missing word that nobody checks, copied a thousand times.” She let that sit. Then she did something her students did not expect. She opened her Bible and read Daniel 7:25 about the power that would think to change times and laws, and then Luke 8:17, that nothing is secret that shall not be made manifest. “But hear me,” she said. “We do not check the record only when it hurts us. We check it when it flatters us too.” And she showed them the same preface, where Diop says Bible passages are copies of Egyptian texts. “That is his claim,” she said. “We check it the same way. We go to the source. And the source of the Scriptures tells us where it came from.” She read 2 Peter 1:21. A young man in the back raised his hand. “So we trust nobody?” “No,” she said. “We trust the Word, and we check everybody else. That is the whole assignment. It is also the whole Christian life.” Every year, a few students came back as seniors and told her that the missing word had changed how they read everything.',
