      body: 'Nia was a first-year law student in Houston, and her moot court partner had picked the strangest practice topic she had ever heard: argue the case Cheikh Anta Diop made in his UNESCO chapter, as if it were a trial. They met on Saturdays in the back room of her aunt’s church, St. James AME, because the wifi was good and the coffee was free. Her partner, Darnell, took the defense; Nia took the prosecution against the old textbooks. She built it the way Diop did, exhibit by exhibit. Exhibit A, human origins in Africa. Exhibit B, the anthropologists whose own numbers, for all their disagreement, leaned one way. Exhibit C, the textbook passage where Diop says the Egyptians were flatly asserted to be white with no research behind it, so that generation after generation was misled. Exhibit D, the melanin samples, and the line where he says he spent two years vainly begging the Cairo Museum for more. Darnell grinned. “Objection. Your own source admits the experts did not all agree.” He slid over the editor’s note printed under the chapter. Nia read it and smiled back. “Read it again. It says not accepted by all. Not refuted. Some accepted it. And the same book gave him the first chapter.” Darnell laughed. “Fine. But you have to concede the homogeneity point. The editor doubts it.” “Conceded,” she said. “It is debated. I will not oversell my case.” Her aunt, Pastor Gloria Mims, had been listening from the doorway with a Bible under her arm. She walked in and set it on the table, open to the eighteenth chapter of Proverbs, and put her finger on verse 17. She said that was exactly what the two of them had just done: one stated the case, and the other came and searched him. “That is how Yahweh wants truth tested,” she said. “In the open. With witnesses.” Nia won the practice round. But what she remembered was Darnell saying on the way out, “You know what convinced me? He left in the line from Herodotus that hurt his own case. Liars do not do that.”',
