      body: 'Dr. Samuel Okafor ran a small genetics lab at a state university in Ohio, and he had been waiting eleven months for a museum in Europe to answer his request. He wanted a few square millimetres of tissue from remains collected during colonial expeditions and stored in a basement for a century. Every letter came back polite and empty. One night he told his wife, Adaeze, that he was thinking of quitting the project. She did not argue. She went to the bookshelf and brought back the UNESCO volume he had bought in graduate school, the one with Cheikh Anta Diop’s chapter at the front. She found the page and handed it to him. Diop had written, decades earlier, that for two years he had been vainly begging the curator of the Cairo Museum for samples to analyse. Samuel read it twice. “Two years,” he said. “He kept asking.” “He kept asking,” Adaeze said, “and he kept working with what he had.” They prayed together that night, the way they had prayed before he defended his dissertation. Adaeze prayed Matthew 10:26 over the work, that nothing covered would stay covered. Samuel prayed something harder: that if his own hypothesis was wrong, he would have the honesty to say so in print, the way Diop had printed Herodotus’ caution against his own case. The next morning he rewrote his request, copied the museum’s own ethics policy into it, and sent it to three more institutions. He also wrote to two colleagues who disagreed with him and asked them to review his method before he published anything. Nine months later the samples came, from a different museum than the one he had first asked. His results confirmed part of what he expected and overturned another part. He published both. In the acknowledgments he thanked his wife, his critics, and a Senegalese physicist who had begged for samples before Samuel was born. And at the bottom, in small type, he wrote the reference his wife had prayed: Matthew 10:26.',
