      body: 'Mrs. Beverly Coleman had never spoken at a city council meeting in her life, but the developer wanted to put a waste transfer station two blocks from the elementary school in her Memphis neighborhood, and somebody had to speak. Her pastor, Rev. Otis Grant of Mount Pisgah, told her the story of Cairo in 1974 at Wednesday Bible study, and he read them the report’s own words: that not everyone had come with research comparable to Diop and Obenga’s, and that there was a real lack of balance in the discussions. “They were outnumbered,” he said, “but they were not outworked.” So Mrs. Coleman went to work. Her grandson pulled the traffic counts. A retired nurse in the choir found the asthma rates by zip code from the county health department. A deacon who drove trucks for thirty years explained the turning radius of a garbage hauler on Elm Street. They put it all in a binder with tabs, one exhibit at a time, and made fifteen copies. The night of the hearing, the developer’s representative gave a ten-minute talk with nice drawings and no numbers. Then Mrs. Coleman stood up with her binder. Her hands shook for the first minute. By the fifth, she was turning tabs like a lawyer. One council member asked her a hard question about property values, and she said honestly that she did not know, and she would find out. That honesty, the council member said afterward, was what made him trust the rest. The vote was postponed for a traffic study. It was not a total win, but it was a fair hearing, and that was what they had prayed for. At Bible study the next Wednesday, Rev. Grant read Deuteronomy 1:17, about hearing the small as well as the great. Mrs. Coleman laughed. “I was the small,” she said. “But I came prepared.” The choir started calling her binder the Cairo Report.',
