  voice: VOICE,
  bigIdea:
    'In The Cultural Unity of Black Africa (French 1959; subtitled, in English, the domains of patriarchy and of matriarchy in classical antiquity) Diop argued that humanity’s civilizations grew up in two early cradles: a southern one, the Nile Valley and Africa, where settled farming life produced matrilineal societies, the territorial state and a collective outlook; and a northern, Eurasian one, where a harsher life produced patriarchy, the city-state and individualism. From this he argued that Europe’s patriarchy was not the universal higher stage every people must reach, and that Africa’s ways with family and kingship were not primitive but different. The record of matrilineal African kingdoms stands; his climate-made binary and his claim that monotheism was born of farming life are weighed and corrected by the Word.',
