      body: 'When Ms. Tanisha Holloway assigned her sixth graders at a charter school in Baltimore a family-tree project, she expected the usual poster boards. Then her student Amara raised her hand and asked, “Can I trace it through my mother’s side only? That is how my grandmother says our people in Ghana do it.” Ms. Holloway said yes, and then she went home and did her own homework. She found that many African kingdoms had passed the crown through the mother’s line, and that a Senegalese scholar named Cheikh Anta Diop had written about it, recording mother-line succession in ancient Ghana and Mali. She also found that Diop had argued with an old European theory that said fathers-only societies were the most advanced. The next week she taught the whole class about it. Amara brought her grandmother, Mrs. Akosua Mensah, who wore kente and told them how, in her family’s tradition, the line runs through the mother. The class was spellbound. Then a boy named DeShawn, whose father was a deacon, raised his hand. “My dad says the Bible says the father is the head. So which one is right?” Ms. Holloway smiled; she had been ready for that one. She did not give them a theory. She opened her Bible and showed them that Yahweh made male and female in His own image in Genesis 1, and that the Ten Commandments say to honour your father and your mother, both of them, in the same sentence. Then she read Proverbs 1:8, where the father’s instruction and the mother’s law stand side by side. “The Bible does not say one family custom makes a people better than another,” she said. “It says honour both, love each other, and follow Yahweh.” Mrs. Mensah nodded slowly. “That is what my mother taught me,” she said. Amara’s project won the school fair. Its title was My Mother’s Line and My Father’s Blessing.',
