  bigIdea:
    'In Precolonial Black Africa (his 1960 doctoral work, in English in 1987) Diop set out to bring Africa’s past back to life from the documents, refusing a history of dates "as dry as laundry lists": he laid out the constitutions of the Mossi and Cayor, mother-line succession in Ghana and Mali, the administration, armies, courts, economies and universities of the African empires, and compared them with Europe’s. His last book, Civilization or Barbarism (French 1981; English 1991, after his death), carried that work into human origins, cultural identity and African philosophy; we know it here only through its publisher’s description and catalogue contents, and we say so. The record of ordered African states stands; his claim to laws that let a people master history is weighed by the Word, which says Yahweh changes the times and sets up kings.',
